On a homeserver, the hard part is not the first install. It is the second one, six months later, when you no longer remember what you typed.

I run OpenBot on my own server, and I deploy it with one Ansible role that lives in my homelab repository. The role makes three decisions, and none of them is specific to OpenBot. That is why I think they are worth copying for the next thing you self-host.

## The role: idempotency is the feature

An Ansible role is a directory contract. You only create the directories you use, and `main.yml` in each one is picked up automatically. For a single-service role, that is usually this:

```text
roles/example/
├── defaults/main.yml     # tunables: user, paths, memory limit
├── tasks/main.yml        # user, files, drop-in, service
├── handlers/main.yml     # daemon-reload + restart
└── meta/main.yml         # optional metadata
```

[Ansible's glossary](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/reference_appendices/glossary.html#term-Idempotency) defines idempotency like this: an operation is idempotent if the result of performing it once is exactly the same as the result of performing it repeatedly. For a service, that means a second run creates no duplicate user, no duplicate unit and no duplicate config.

It also means no pointless restarts, and this is where people overreach. The role does not avoid restarts by being clever. It avoids them because restarts live in **handlers**. A [handler](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_handlers.html) only runs when a task that notifies it reports `changed`, and if several tasks notify the same handler, it still runs once. A run that changes nothing restarts nothing.

My acceptance test is a dry run followed by running the role twice:

```bash
ansible-playbook site.yml --check --diff   # what would change
ansible-playbook site.yml                  # first run: applies the changes
ansible-playbook site.yml                  # second run: expect changed=0
```

If the second run reports changes, something in the role is not declarative yet.

## A dedicated user, and not in the `docker` group

OpenBot gets its own system account. The important part is what that account does **not** have: membership in the `docker` group.

This is not a convenience flag. Docker's own docs ([post-install](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/linux-postinstall/) and [security](https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/)) are blunt about it: the daemon always runs as root, only trusted users should control it, and the `docker` group grants root-level privileges. Anyone who can talk to the daemon can start a container with the host's `/` mounted into it, so access to the daemon is effectively access to the host.

A service that never needs the Docker API should not hold that. So the service account is not in `docker`. If a container runtime is involved anywhere in the setup, the play drives it, not this account.

Here is the task, written generically:

```yaml
- name: Create the service account (no supplementary groups)
  ansible.builtin.user:
    name: example
    system: true
    create_home: true
    shell: /usr/sbin/nologin
    groups: ""
    state: present
```

Two details in the [`user` module](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/latest/collections/ansible/builtin/user_module.html) matter here:

- I set `groups: ""`. Leaving the key out would not mean "no extra groups"; it would mean Ansible does not manage groups at all, and an account someone had already added to `docker` would stay there. Once you list `groups`, Ansible manages membership and by default removes the user from every supplementary group you did not list (the primary group is not affected). The empty string removes all of them, so the account converges to "no extra groups" even if someone added it to `docker` by hand.
- `system: true` cannot be changed on an existing user. Creating the account correctly the first time is part of idempotency.

The trade-off: file ownership and permissions have to be right from the start, and anything the service needs from a privileged subsystem has to be arranged outside that account. I consider that a feature, not a cost.

## The memory cap, as a systemd drop-in

I do not want a misbehaving service to take the whole homeserver down with it, so the role caps its memory at the systemd layer, next to the thing that already starts it.

[`MemoryMax=`](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.resource-control.html) sets an absolute limit on a unit (available since systemd 231). If the unit's processes cannot stay under it, the OOM killer is invoked inside that unit. So it does not prevent the OOM killer: it is what triggers it, but only for that unit, which is exactly the blast radius I want. systemd's docs call it the last line of defense; the softer main control is `MemoryHigh=`, which throttles and reclaims instead of killing. Pick that one if you want slowdown rather than a kill.

The limit goes in a drop-in instead of an edit to the unit file:

```plaintext
# /etc/systemd/system/example.service.d/10-memory.conf
[Service]
MemoryMax=1G
```

`1G` is a placeholder here, not my real value. Size it for your own machine; the directive accepts `K`, `M`, `G` and `T` suffixes, or a percentage of installed RAM.

Why a drop-in? Files in `<unit>.d/*.conf` are [merged](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html) in alphanumeric order and parsed after the main unit file, and drop-ins in `/etc` take precedence over those in `/run` and `/usr/lib`. systemd's docs spell out the benefit: when you override only the settings you care about, vendor updates to the rest of the unit still apply. If you replace the whole unit, you stop receiving those improvements. In practice, an upgrade that ships a new unit does not silently drop my limit, and the limit lives in the role instead of in a file I edited by hand once.

After writing the drop-in, systemd has to re-read its configuration with `systemctl daemon-reload`. That is a job for the handler, so it only happens when the drop-in actually changed:

```yaml
# tasks/main.yml
- name: Install the memory-cap drop-in
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    dest: /etc/systemd/system/example.service.d/10-memory.conf
    mode: "0644"
    content: |
      [Service]
      MemoryMax=1G
  notify: Reload systemd and restart example

# handlers/main.yml
- name: Reload systemd and restart example
  ansible.builtin.systemd_service:
    name: example
    state: restarted
    daemon_reload: true
```

Once it is applied, `systemctl show example -p MemoryMax` is a quick way to check the limit systemd actually applied.

## A checklist for the next service

None of this is OpenBot-specific. When I turn any self-hosted app into a managed service, I go through this list:

- One role per service, with `defaults/` holding every tunable.
- One dedicated system user, no supplementary groups, no login shell.
- Config and secrets owned by that user, with modes set explicitly.
- A unit the role installs, or a packaged unit the role only extends.
- Limits and overrides as drop-ins under `/etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/`.
- Handlers for `daemon-reload` and restart, so a no-op run stays a no-op.
- A second run of the role as the acceptance test: it should change nothing.

That is the whole pattern. It is not exciting, and that is the point: a service I can rebuild by re-running one role, under an account that cannot own the host, with a memory ceiling that survives upgrades.
